; ct.rules — the derived state category, given its own vocabulary.
;
; A model's meaning IS a category (kernel Appendix I.1): objects are the
; reachable situations, morphisms are move sequences, composition is
; concatenation, identities are "do nothing". `pol check` asks four questions
; about it — `possible`, `never`, `live` and `inevitable` — and the extension is
; explicit that those three are the common case rather than the instrument
; (extension §2). This file is the instrument's vocabulary: the structure of
; that category, named, so a model can be asked what the four modalities have
; no spelling for.
;
; WHERE IT LIVES. It is a RULES library — it declares relations, never types —
; so `(load "ct.rules")` belongs in a .rules file and nowhere else. Loading it
; into a .pol model would be accepted and useless: nothing here is a declaration
; a model's universe can hold.
;
; THE COST, stated as stdlib.pol states its own. Ten relation names: a .rules
; file that loads this one gives up `reach`, `mutual`, `recurrent`, `before`,
; `phase-before`, `one-way`, `escapes`, `final-phase`, `moves` and `dead-end`,
; and may not redeclare any of them — which is exactly what the corpus's
; existing files do with the first, and why the migration is a deletion.
;
; WHAT IS A PAIR AND WHAT IS NOT, which is the one thing to know before writing
; against this file. `phase` and `phase-step` are BUILT-IN (extension §2): the
; interrogator quotients the state category by mutual reachability in one linear
; pass, so everything defined from them is linear too. `reach` and `before` are
; not, and cannot be made so — their ANSWERS are sets of pairs, quadratic in the
; situation count however they are computed. Ask a linear question wherever one
; will do; §7's goal forms are written so that the common ones are.

; 1. Hom-sets — the free category on the reachable graph
;
; `reach S T` is the hom-set of the state category made a relation: non-empty
; exactly when some move sequence runs from S to T. The first rule is the
; identity morphism, the second is composition — the free category, stated as
; the two clauses that generate it.
;
; No `init` premise is needed and none is wanted: the built-ins range over
; REACHABLE situations only (§12.3), so the space the engine enumerated is
; already the space that exists.
;
; USE IT LAST. Nothing else in this file needs it any more, and that is
; deliberate: materialising the closure costs a tuple per ordered pair of
; situations, which measured at 1 938 situations does not finish in 100 seconds,
; while the space itself is built in 30 milliseconds. It is kept because the
; hom-set is what the category IS, and a model small enough to want it whole
; should be able to ask. Where the question is "can S still get to an F" — which
; is nearly always the question — §7's `can-reach` answers it by walking
; backwards from F instead, in one pass over the edges.
(relation reach (Situation Situation))
(rule (reach S S) (situation S))
(rule (reach S T) (edge E S M) (reach M T))

; 2. Isomorphism — the maximal subgroupoid
;
; Two situations are isomorphic in the state category when each reaches the
; other. That relation's classes are what `phase` names: the interrogator
; computes them directly, and `(phase S P)` answers with P, the least-indexed
; situation of S's class. The categorical reading is the one worth keeping,
; because it says what a class MEANS — inside one, nothing has been spent; every
; arrangement in it is recoverable from every other.
;
; `mutual` is that equivalence spelled out as pairs, for the questions that want
; them. Note the shape of the cost: the first literal generates each situation
; with its phase, the second generates only the members of THAT phase, so the
; work is the answer's own size rather than a scan of all pairs.
(relation mutual (Situation Situation))
(rule (mutual S T) (phase S P) (phase T P))

; `recurrent S` — S lies on a cycle: some move leaves S and comes back, perhaps
; the long way round. Equivalently S's phase is not a single spent point.
;
; WHY IT EARNS ITS PLACE. It is the question `mutual` is usually wanted for, and
; the one that could not be written before `phase` existed: "S and T are in one
; class and are not the same situation" needs an inequality on situations, and
; there is none — the language compares a cell against a value, never one atom
; against another. Reading it off an edge that stays inside the phase avoids the
; comparison entirely. It is also the shape of every non-termination question:
; a model that can run forever without arriving is one with a recurrent
; situation the goal does not hold in.
(relation recurrent (Situation))
(rule (recurrent S) (edge E S T) (phase S P) (phase T P))

; 3. The posetal reflection — what a move costs
;
; Quotient the state category by §2 and what is left is a POSET: the same order,
; every cycle collapsed to a point. That quotient is exactly the `phase-step`
; graph, and it is acyclic by construction — a step between two phases can never
; be walked back, or they would have been one phase.
;
; WHY IT EARNS ITS PLACE. This is what most models are actually for, and it is
; the question the modalities cannot put. `live G` failing says a trap exists
; and prints one route into it; `before` is the whole trap structure, and it is
; asked WITHOUT naming a goal — which is what makes it answerable of a model
; nobody has yet written the claims for.
;
; `phase-before` is the order on phases; `before` is the same order read back
; down onto situations. Written in that order on purpose: bind the phase pair
; first and each situation pair is generated once, where binding the situations
; first would scan every pair of situations to test each.
(relation phase-before (Situation Situation))
(rule (phase-before P Q) (phase-step P Q))
(rule (phase-before P R) (phase-step P Q) (phase-before Q R))

(relation before (Situation Situation))
(rule (before S T) (phase-before P Q) (phase S P) (phase T Q))

; 4. One-way doors — irreversibility, per move
;
; §3 in the form an author can act on: a NAMED move, and the situation it leaves
; for good. §15 reports which moves can break a law; this reports which moves
; break a return. A model with no one-way rows is fully reversible, and that is
; worth being able to state rather than believe.
;
; A move is one-way exactly when it crosses out of its phase: if the target
; could come back, the two ends would share a phase. So this needs no closure
; and no negation — one literal per end, and the crossing test the built-in
; supplies.
(relation one-way (Edge Situation Situation))
(rule (one-way E S T) (edge E S T) (phase S P) (phase T Q) (phase-step P Q))

; 5. Final phases — where a model ends up
;
; A situation in a TERMINAL class of §3's poset. It may still move, perhaps
; forever, but everything it reaches reaches it back: nothing further is
; decided. Endgames, absorbed states, the emergency nobody can lift.
;
; `escapes` is scaffolding rather than vocabulary, and is named instead of
; hidden because it costs a name either way: a variable inside `not` must be
; bound elsewhere in the body (range restriction, extension §1), so "there is no
; phase after this one" has to become a relation before it can be negated.
(relation escapes (Situation))
(rule (escapes S) (phase S P) (phase-step P Q))
(relation final-phase (Situation))
(rule (final-phase S) (situation S) (not (escapes S)))

; 6. Dead ends
;
; A situation with no move at all — the degenerate final phase, a class of one
; object carrying nothing but its identity. §15 already reports these, and the
; reason to have them HERE is that a relation JOINS: "which one-way move leads
; straight into one" is then a query, instead of two reports read side by side.
(relation moves (Situation))
(rule (moves S) (edge E S T))
(relation dead-end (Situation))
(rule (dead-end S) (situation S) (not (moves S)))

; 7. Goals — the modalities, as three composable lines
;
; §1–6 ask what a model's shape IS, and need no goal to do it. This section is
; the other half, and it needs one: a guard arrives as a form slot, so the shape
; every scenario in the corpus writes by hand becomes one line, with only the
; guard left to write.
;
; Written out, the river crossing's whole question:
;
;   (satisfies intact     (and (is farmer.at right) (is wolf.at right)
;                              (is goat.at right)   (is cabbage.at right)))
;   (can-reach  finishable intact)
;   (trapped    blunders   finishable)
;
; POLARITY, which is what these are most worth having for, because every file in
; the corpus carries a hand-written warning about getting it wrong:
;
;   possible F   `satisfies` IS the satisfying set     non-empty ⟺ holds
;   never F      `satisfies` of the FORBIDDEN guard    empty     ⟺ holds
;   live F       `trapped` is the COUNTEREXAMPLE set   empty     ⟺ holds
;
; A `live` question inverts once and inverts silently: name `can-reach`'s result
; as the answer and it reads as a satisfying set, so every verdict comes out
; backwards while the suite stays green. Spending a name on `trapped` is what
; makes the complement visible — taken by a form that says so, rather than by a
; rule the reader has to check.
;
; WHY THREE FORMS AND NOT ONE. A form cannot invent a name, so a combined
; `(live-set X G)` would have to be handed the two intermediates anyway. Three
; also lets a scenario stop after the first — `possible` needs only `satisfies`
; — and lets the middle relation carry a name of the model's own, which is what
; a witness prints.
;
; `can-reach` is a BACKWARD walk: the goal set, then anything with a move into
; what is already in. It used to be written `(rule (C S) (reach S T) (R T))`,
; which is the same set and asks for the whole hom-set to compute it — the
; closure is quadratic, this is one pass over the edges, and every `live`
; cross-check in the corpus goes through here. The rewrite is the reason §1 now
; says "use it last".
(form (satisfies R G)
  (relation R 1)
  (rule (R S) (situation S) (holds S G)))

(form (can-reach C R)
  (relation C 1)
  (rule (C S) (R S))
  (rule (C S) (edge E S T) (C T)))

(form (trapped X C)
  (relation X 1)
  (rule (X S) (situation S) (not (C S))))

; WHAT IS NOT HERE, and why. One limit, and it is mechanical.
;
; THE ARROW PROPERTIES OF A SCHEMA. An arrow being injective or surjective, a
; span being the whole pullback and not merely a sound part of it — these belong
; to this file by subject, and cannot be written in it. A rule reads an arrow by
; naming it inside a chain (`X.stands`), and a form substitutes only a path's
; ROOT segment (`Expander.subst`, and deliberately: it is why `maybe` sugars a
; whole declaration rather than part of one). So no template can be generic over
; the arrow, and each such question stays one rule per arrow, written where the
; arrow is known — the model's own .rules file.
;
; What the kernel refuses to a LAW is a second, independent limit worth knowing
; beside it: §10.2 decides the right of `is` lexically, so two bound variables
; cannot be compared in a guard at all. That is why injectivity is a rules
; question and can never be an `equation`.
